```server-go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "log"

    "github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-go/v7/appwrite"
)

func main() {
    client := appwrite.NewClient(
        appwrite.WithEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"),
        appwrite.WithProject("<PROJECT_ID>"),
        appwrite.WithKey("<API_KEY>"),
    )

    analytics := appwrite.NewAnalytics(client)

    countries, err := analytics.ListMetrics(
        "<PROPERTY_ID>",
        analytics.WithListMetricsDimensions([]string{"country"}),
        analytics.WithListMetricsDateRange("7d"),
        analytics.WithListMetricsLimit(5),
    )
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }

    for _, row := range countries.Metrics {
        fmt.Println(*row.Value, row.Visitors)
    }
}
```
```server-rust
use appwrite::Client;
use appwrite::enums::AnalyticsDimension;
use appwrite::services::Analytics;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let client = Client::new();
    client.set_endpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1");
    client.set_project("<PROJECT_ID>");
    client.set_key("<API_KEY>");

    let analytics = Analytics::new(&client);

    let countries = analytics.list_metrics(
        "<PROPERTY_ID>",
        None, // queries
        None, // interval
        Some(vec![AnalyticsDimension::Country]),
        Some("7d"),
        None, // start_at
        None, // end_at
        Some(5),
        None, // total
    ).await?;

    for row in countries.metrics {
        println!("{} {}", row.value.unwrap_or_default(), row.visitors);
    }

    Ok(())
}
```
```bash
appwrite client \
    --endpoint https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1 \
    --project-id '<PROJECT_ID>' \
    --key '<API_KEY>'

appwrite analytics list-metrics \
    --property-id '<PROPERTY_ID>' \
    --dimensions country \
    --date-range 7d \
    --limit 5
```
```curl
curl -G 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/metrics' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>' \
    --data-urlencode 'dimensions[]=country' \
    --data-urlencode 'dateRange=7d' \
    --data-urlencode 'limit=5'
```
{% /multicode %}

## Response shapes {% #shapes %}

`listMetrics` returns one of three shapes, depending on whether you pass `dimensions` or `interval`. You can't pass both.

| You pass | You get | Fields |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Neither | One row with totals for the whole range | Every metric on this page |
| `dimensions` | One row per value, ranked by visitors (`eventName` by events), with `value` set | `visitors`, `sessions`, and `events` |
| `interval` | One row per time bucket, oldest first, with `date` set | `visitors`, `sessions`, and `events` |

Without `dimensions` or `interval`, the single row looks like this:

```json
{
    "total": 1,
    "metrics": [
        {
            "value": null,
            "date": null,
            "visitors": 14212,
            "sessions": 22603,
            "pageviews": 63207,
            "events": 144886,
            "visits": 22601,
            "bounceRate": 46.9,
            "visitDuration": 77.01,
            "viewsPerVisit": 2.79,
            "scrollDepth": 61.3,
            "engagementTime": 75
        }
    ]
}
```

`visitDuration` and `engagementTime` are in seconds. `bounceRate` and `scrollDepth` are percentages.

`sessions` and `visits` count visits in two ways. `sessions` counts every visit with an event in the range. `visits` counts only the visits that started in it, like **Visits** on the dashboard. They differ when a visit started before the range.

## Parameters {% #parameters %}

| Parameter | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `dimensions` | One dimension to break down by, from the [breakdowns](#breakdowns) table. |
| `interval` | The bucket size for a time series: `1h`, `1d`, `1w`, or `1m`. Buckets start in UTC. |
| `dateRange` | The length of the range in days, such as `7d`. Defaults to `30d`. Any other format, such as `2w`, also falls back to `30d`. |
| `startAt`, `endAt` | Exact bounds in ISO 8601. Times without an offset are UTC. With both, they override `dateRange`. With only `startAt`, the range ends now. With only `endAt`, the range is `dateRange` long and ends at `endAt`. |
| `queries` | Up to 10 filters. See [Filter metrics](#filters). |
| `limit` | The number of rows a breakdown returns, from 1 to 500. Defaults to 100. |

A range never starts before your plan's [data retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits#retention).

## Filter metrics {% #filters %}

Filters narrow the events every metric is calculated from. Each filter is a query on a dimension, using `equal`, `notEqual`, `contains`, `startsWith`, or `endsWith`. This example returns daily visitors who arrived from search engines over the last two weeks.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
import { Client, Analytics, AnalyticsInterval, Query } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const analytics = new Analytics(client);

const days = await analytics.listMetrics({
    propertyId: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
    interval: AnalyticsInterval.OneDay,
    dateRange: '14d',
    queries: [Query.equal('channel', 'search')],
});

for (const row of days.metrics) {
    console.log(row.date, row.visitors);
}
```
```server-deno
import { Client, Analytics, AnalyticsInterval, Query } from 'npm:node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
